<!-- SECTION 1: WHAT IS DNS -->
<section id="what">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(56,189,248,0.12);color:var(--accent);">🌐</span> What is DNS & Why It Exists</h2>

  <p>The <span class="highlight">Domain Name System (DNS)</span> is a globally distributed, hierarchical database that translates human-readable domain names (like <span class="highlight2">google.com</span>) into machine-readable IP addresses (like <span class="highlight4">142.250.190.78</span>). It is one of the most critical pieces of internet infrastructure — without it, you'd have to memorize IP addresses for every website.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    Think of DNS as the internet's phone book. You know a person's name (domain), and you look up their phone number (IP address) to call them. But unlike a single book, DNS is a distributed system with millions of "phone books" around the world, organized in a strict hierarchy.
  </div>

  <h3>What DNS Actually Does</h3>

  <div class="card accent-border">
    <p><span class="highlight">Forward Lookup (Name → IP):</span> Converts <code>www.example.com</code> → <code>93.184.216.34</code>. This is what happens every time you type a URL in your browser.</p>
    <p><span class="highlight3">Reverse Lookup (IP → Name):</span> Converts <code>93.184.216.34</code> → <code>www.example.com</code>. Used for security logging, email verification, and diagnostics. Uses PTR records.</p>
    <p><span class="highlight4">Mail Routing:</span> DNS tells email systems where to deliver mail for a domain via MX records.</p>
    <p><span class="highlight5">Service Discovery:</span> SRV records help locate specific services (VoIP, LDAP, etc.) on a network.</p>
    <p><span class="highlight2">Verification & Authentication:</span> TXT records store SPF, DKIM, DMARC data for email security and domain ownership verification.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Key Characteristics</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <dl class="key-value">
      <dt>Distributed</dt>
      <dd>No single server holds all DNS data. It's spread across millions of servers worldwide.</dd>
      <dt>Hierarchical</dt>
      <dd>Organized in a tree structure: Root → TLD → Domain → Subdomain.</dd>
      <dt>Redundant</dt>
      <dd>Multiple servers at every level ensure no single point of failure.</dd>
      <dt>Cached</dt>
      <dd>Results are cached at every level (browser, OS, ISP) to reduce load and speed up lookups.</dd>
      <dt>Port</dt>
      <dd>DNS uses <strong>UDP port 53</strong> for queries and <strong>TCP port 53</strong> for zone transfers and large responses.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 2: HOW DNS WORKS -->
<section id="how">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(129,140,248,0.12);color:var(--accent2);">⚙️</span> How DNS Works — Step by Step</h2>

  <p>When you type <span class="highlight2">www.google.com</span> in your browser, here's the complete journey a DNS query takes:</p>

  <ol class="step-list">
    <li><strong>Browser Cache Check:</strong> Your browser first checks its own local DNS cache. If you visited google.com recently, the IP might already be stored.</li>
    <li><strong>OS Cache Check (Stub Resolver):</strong> If not in browser cache, the OS-level resolver checks its cache. On Linux, you can check <code>/etc/hosts</code> file too — entries here override DNS.</li>
    <li><strong>Recursive Resolver (ISP / Configured DNS):</strong> If not cached locally, the query goes to your configured DNS server (your ISP's resolver, or something like 8.8.8.8 / 1.1.1.1). This is the <span class="highlight">Recursive Resolver</span> — it does the hard work on your behalf.</li>
    <li><strong>Root Server Query:</strong> The recursive resolver asks one of the 13 <span class="highlight3">Root Name Servers</span>: "Where can I find .com?" The root server responds with the address of the .com TLD servers.</li>
    <li><strong>TLD Server Query:</strong> The resolver asks the <span class="highlight5">.com TLD server</span>: "Where can I find google.com?" The TLD server responds with the address of Google's authoritative name servers.</li>
    <li><strong>Authoritative Name Server Query:</strong> The resolver asks <span class="highlight4">Google's authoritative name server</span>: "What is the IP for www.google.com?" The authoritative server responds with the final answer: the A record (e.g., <code>142.250.190.78</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Response Returned:</strong> The recursive resolver caches the result (for the TTL duration) and returns it to your OS, which caches it, then to your browser, which caches it, and finally connects to the IP.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Visual Flow</h3>
  <div class="flow-diagram">
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--accent2);">🖥️<br>Browser</div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--accent2);">💻<br>OS Stub<br>Resolver</div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--accent);">🔄<br>Recursive<br>Resolver</div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--accent3);">🌍<br>Root<br>Server</div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--accent5);">📁<br>TLD<br>Server</div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
    <div class="flow-box" style="border-color:var(--accent4);">✅<br>Authoritative<br>NS</div>
  </div>

  <div class="card accent2-border">
    <p class="card-title" style="color:var(--accent2);">⚡ Iterative vs Recursive Queries</p>
    <p><span class="highlight2">Recursive Query:</span> Your computer sends one query to the recursive resolver and expects a final answer. The resolver does all the chasing around. Your computer says: "Get me the answer, I'll wait."</p>
    <p><span class="highlight">Iterative Query:</span> The recursive resolver makes iterative queries to root → TLD → authoritative servers. Each server says: "I don't know the full answer, but try asking this server next." The resolver follows the chain.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 3: RIGHT TO LEFT -->
<section id="right-to-left">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(244,114,182,0.12);color:var(--accent3);">🔄</span> DNS Reads Right-to-Left</h2>

  <p>This is a crucial concept that confuses many people. <span class="highlight3">DNS processes domain names from RIGHT to LEFT</span>, even though we read and type them left to right.</p>

  <h3>Forward Lookup — Reading Right to Left</h3>

  <p>Consider the fully qualified domain name (FQDN): <span class="highlight">www.mail.google.com.</span> (yes, there's a trailing dot!)</p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">dns-parsing.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="section-head">How DNS reads:  www.mail.google.com.</span>

<span class="comment"># The trailing dot represents the ROOT zone</span>
<span class="comment"># DNS resolution order (RIGHT → LEFT):</span>

<span class="key">Step 1:</span>  <span class="val">.</span>           ← Root zone (the trailing dot)
<span class="key">Step 2:</span>  <span class="val">com.</span>        ← TLD (Top-Level Domain)
<span class="key">Step 3:</span>  <span class="val">google.com.</span> ← Second-Level Domain (the registered domain)
<span class="key">Step 4:</span>  <span class="val">mail.google.com.</span>  ← Subdomain
<span class="key">Step 5:</span>  <span class="val">www.mail.google.com.</span> ← Host / further subdomain

<span class="comment"># The hierarchy from the DNS perspective:</span>
<span class="comment">#   .  →  com  →  google  →  mail  →  www</span>
<span class="comment"># (root) (TLD)  (domain) (sub)   (host)</span></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Reverse Lookup — Reading Left to Right</h3>

  <p>For reverse DNS (IP → Name), the IP address is <span class="highlight3">reversed and placed under the special <code>in-addr.arpa</code> domain</span>. This flips the reading direction to LEFT to RIGHT, matching the DNS hierarchy:</p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">reverse-dns.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="section-head">Reverse DNS for IP: 192.168.1.100</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Reverse the octets</span>
<span class="key">Original IP:</span>  <span class="val">192.168.1.100</span>
<span class="key">Reversed:</span>     <span class="val">100.1.168.192</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Append .in-addr.arpa</span>
<span class="key">PTR Lookup:</span>   <span class="val">100.1.168.192.in-addr.arpa.</span>

<span class="comment"># Why reverse? So the DNS hierarchy works naturally:</span>
<span class="comment">#   arpa → in-addr → 192 → 168 → 1 → 100</span>
<span class="comment"># This mirrors how IP networks are delegated:</span>
<span class="comment">#   192.x.x.x is a /8 block</span>
<span class="comment">#   192.168.x.x is a /16 block</span>
<span class="comment">#   192.168.1.x is a /24 block</span>
<span class="comment">#   192.168.1.100 is a specific host</span>

<span class="comment"># For IPv6, each hex digit is reversed individually:</span>
<span class="key">IPv6:</span>   <span class="val">2001:0db8::1</span>
<span class="key">PTR:</span>    <span class="val">1.0.0.0...8.b.d.0.1.0.0.2.ip6.arpa.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="analogy">
    Think of it like a postal address. You write: "123 Main St, Springfield, IL, USA". But the postal system processes it from the broadest to the most specific: USA → IL → Springfield → Main St → 123. DNS works the same way — it starts from the broadest category (root/TLD) and narrows down.
  </div>
</section>

