<!-- SECTION 4: RECORD OVERVIEW -->
<section id="records">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(52,211,153,0.12);color:var(--accent4);">📋</span> DNS Record Types — Overview</h2>

  <p>DNS records (also called <span class="highlight">Resource Records or RRs</span>) are entries in a DNS zone file that provide information about a domain. Each record has a specific type and purpose.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Record</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example Value</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight">A</span></td><td>Maps domain to IPv4 address</td><td>93.184.216.34</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight">AAAA</span></td><td>Maps domain to IPv6 address</td><td>2606:2800:220:1::</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight3">SOA</span></td><td>Start of Authority — zone metadata</td><td>ns1.example.com. admin.example.com. 2024010101 ...</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight2">NS</span></td><td>Delegates zone to name server</td><td>ns1.google.com.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight5">PTR</span></td><td>Reverse lookup (IP → domain)</td><td>host.example.com.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight4">MX</span></td><td>Mail exchange server</td><td>10 mail.example.com.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight">CNAME</span></td><td>Alias / canonical name</td><td>www.example.com → example.com</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight2">TXT</span></td><td>Arbitrary text (SPF, DKIM, etc.)</td><td>"v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com ~all"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight3">SRV</span></td><td>Service locator</td><td>0 5 5060 sip.example.com.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="highlight5">CAA</span></td><td>Certificate Authority Authorization</td><td>0 issue "letsencrypt.org"</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 5: A RECORD -->
<section id="a-record">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(56,189,248,0.12);color:var(--accent);">🅰️</span> A Record (Address Record)</h2>

  <p>The <span class="highlight">A record</span> is the most fundamental DNS record. It maps a domain name to an <span class="highlight4">IPv4 address</span> (32-bit, like <code>142.250.190.78</code>).</p>

  <div class="card accent-border">
    <p class="card-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Format</p>
    <dl class="key-value">
      <dt>Name</dt><dd>The domain or hostname (e.g., <code>www</code>, <code>@</code> for root)</dd>
      <dt>TTL</dt><dd>Time-to-live in seconds — how long to cache this record</dd>
      <dt>Class</dt><dd>Almost always <code>IN</code> (Internet)</dd>
      <dt>Type</dt><dd><code>A</code></dd>
      <dt>Value</dt><dd>An IPv4 address (e.g., <code>93.184.216.34</code>)</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">zone-file-example.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="comment">; A record examples in a zone file</span>
<span class="key">@</span>       <span class="val">300</span>   IN   A   <span class="cmd">93.184.216.34</span>        <span class="comment">; root domain</span>
<span class="key">www</span>     <span class="val">300</span>   IN   A   <span class="cmd">93.184.216.34</span>        <span class="comment">; www subdomain</span>
<span class="key">api</span>     <span class="val">60</span>    IN   A   <span class="cmd">10.0.1.50</span>            <span class="comment">; api subdomain (short TTL)</span>
<span class="key">blog</span>    <span class="val">3600</span>  IN   A   <span class="cmd">198.51.100.22</span>        <span class="comment">; blog on different server</span>

<span class="comment">; Multiple A records = Round-robin load balancing</span>
<span class="key">app</span>     <span class="val">60</span>    IN   A   <span class="cmd">10.0.1.10</span>
<span class="key">app</span>     <span class="val">60</span>    IN   A   <span class="cmd">10.0.1.11</span>
<span class="key">app</span>     <span class="val">60</span>    IN   A   <span class="cmd">10.0.1.12</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>When multiple A records exist for the same name, DNS returns them all and the client (or resolver) picks one — this is basic <span class="highlight">round-robin load balancing</span>.</p>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 6: AAAA -->
<section id="aaaa">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(129,140,248,0.12);color:var(--accent2);">6️⃣</span> AAAA Record (IPv6)</h2>

  <p>The <span class="highlight2">AAAA record</span> (called "quad-A") is identical in function to the A record, but maps to an <span class="highlight2">IPv6 address</span> (128-bit) instead of IPv4. As IPv4 addresses run out, AAAA records become increasingly important.</p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">aaaa-example.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="key">@</span>     <span class="val">300</span>   IN   AAAA   <span class="cmd">2606:2800:0220:0001:0248:1893:25c8:1946</span>
<span class="key">www</span>   <span class="val">300</span>   IN   AAAA   <span class="cmd">2606:2800:220:1:248:1893:25c8:1946</span>

<span class="comment">; Why "AAAA"? Because IPv6 addresses are 4× longer</span>
<span class="comment">; than IPv4 (128 bits vs 32 bits), hence 4× A = AAAA</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 7: SOA -->
<section id="soa">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(244,114,182,0.12);color:var(--accent3);">👑</span> SOA Record (Start of Authority)</h2>

  <p>The <span class="highlight3">SOA record</span> is the king of a DNS zone. Every DNS zone <strong>must</strong> have exactly one SOA record. It defines the zone's primary authority and contains critical metadata for zone management, especially <span class="highlight">zone transfers</span> between primary and secondary DNS servers.</p>

  <div class="card accent3-border">
    <p class="card-title" style="color:var(--accent3);">SOA Record Fields</p>
    <dl class="key-value">
      <dt>MNAME</dt><dd>Primary (master) name server for the zone</dd>
      <dt>RNAME</dt><dd>Email of the zone admin (the first <code>.</code> replaces <code>@</code>, so <code>admin.example.com</code> = <code>admin@example.com</code>)</dd>
      <dt>SERIAL</dt><dd>Zone version number. Incremented on every change. Secondary servers use this to detect updates. Common format: <code>YYYYMMDDnn</code></dd>
      <dt>REFRESH</dt><dd>How often (seconds) secondary servers check for updates from primary</dd>
      <dt>RETRY</dt><dd>How long (seconds) secondary waits before retrying a failed refresh</dd>
      <dt>EXPIRE</dt><dd>How long (seconds) secondary considers its data valid if it can't reach primary. After this, it stops answering queries.</dd>
      <dt>MINIMUM (TTL)</dt><dd>The negative cache TTL — how long resolvers cache "this domain doesn't exist" (NXDOMAIN) responses</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">soa-record.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="comment">; SOA record anatomy:</span>
<span class="key">example.com.</span>  <span class="val">86400</span>  IN  SOA  <span class="cmd">ns1.example.com.</span>  <span class="cmd">admin.example.com.</span> (
                    <span class="val">2024031501</span>  <span class="comment">; Serial   (YYYYMMDD + revision number)</span>
                    <span class="val">3600</span>        <span class="comment">; Refresh  (1 hour — check for updates)</span>
                    <span class="val">900</span>         <span class="comment">; Retry    (15 min — retry on failure)</span>
                    <span class="val">1209600</span>     <span class="comment">; Expire   (2 weeks — stop if unreachable)</span>
                    <span class="val">86400</span>       <span class="comment">; Minimum  (1 day — negative cache TTL)</span>
                )</div>
  </div>

  <div class="analogy">
    The SOA record is like the title page and publishing info of a book. It tells you who wrote it (MNAME), who to contact (RNAME), what edition it is (SERIAL), and how frequently it should be checked for new editions (REFRESH).
  </div>

  <h3>Why the Serial Number Matters</h3>
  <p>When a secondary DNS server does a zone transfer (AXFR/IXFR), it compares its local serial number with the primary's serial. If the primary's serial is higher, it downloads the updated zone. If you edit a zone file but forget to increment the serial, <span class="highlight3">secondary servers will NOT pick up the changes</span>.</p>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 8: NS -->
<section id="ns">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(251,191,36,0.12);color:var(--accent5);">🏷️</span> NS Record (Name Server)</h2>

  <p>The <span class="highlight5">NS record</span> delegates a DNS zone to a specific authoritative name server. Every domain must have at least two NS records for redundancy. NS records are how the DNS hierarchy works — they are the "signposts" that point you down the tree.</p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">ns-records.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="comment">; NS records for google.com</span>
<span class="key">google.com.</span>   <span class="val">86400</span>   IN   NS   <span class="cmd">ns1.google.com.</span>
<span class="key">google.com.</span>   <span class="val">86400</span>   IN   NS   <span class="cmd">ns2.google.com.</span>
<span class="key">google.com.</span>   <span class="val">86400</span>   IN   NS   <span class="cmd">ns3.google.com.</span>
<span class="key">google.com.</span>   <span class="val">86400</span>   IN   NS   <span class="cmd">ns4.google.com.</span>

<span class="comment">; Subdomain delegation</span>
<span class="key">dev.google.com.</span>  <span class="val">86400</span>  IN  NS  <span class="cmd">ns1.dev.google.com.</span>

<span class="comment">; Glue records (needed when NS is within the same zone)</span>
<span class="key">ns1.google.com.</span>  <span class="val">86400</span>  IN  A   <span class="cmd">216.239.32.10</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="card accent5-border">
    <p class="card-title" style="color:var(--accent5);">🔗 Glue Records</p>
    <p>If <code>google.com</code>'s name servers are <code>ns1.google.com</code> and <code>ns2.google.com</code>, you have a chicken-and-egg problem: to find the IP of <code>ns1.google.com</code>, you'd need to query <code>google.com</code>'s name server... which IS <code>ns1.google.com</code>. <span class="highlight5">Glue records</span> solve this by providing A records for the name servers directly at the parent zone (the .com TLD servers).</p>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 9: PTR -->
<section id="ptr">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(52,211,153,0.12);color:var(--accent4);">🔙</span> PTR Record (Pointer / Reverse DNS)</h2>

  <p>The <span class="highlight4">PTR record</span> does the opposite of an A record — it maps an <span class="highlight">IP address back to a domain name</span>. It's stored in the special <code>in-addr.arpa</code> zone (for IPv4) or <code>ip6.arpa</code> zone (for IPv6).</p>

  <h3>How PTR Records Work</h3>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">ptr-explained.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="section-head">Reverse DNS Lookup for 142.250.190.78</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Reverse the IP octets</span>
   <span class="val">142.250.190.78</span>  →  <span class="val">78.190.250.142</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Append .in-addr.arpa.</span>
   <span class="cmd">78.190.250.142.in-addr.arpa.</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Query for PTR record at that name</span>
   <span class="key">78.190.250.142.in-addr.arpa.</span>  IN  PTR  <span class="cmd">maa03s44-in-f14.1e100.net.</span>

<span class="comment"># The zone delegation mirrors the IP allocation:</span>
<span class="comment">#   arpa.  ← managed by IANA</span>
<span class="comment">#     in-addr.arpa.  ← managed by IANA</span>
<span class="comment">#       142.in-addr.arpa.  ← delegated to IP block owner</span>
<span class="comment">#         250.142.in-addr.arpa.  ← further delegated</span></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Why PTR Records Matter</h3>
  <div class="card accent4-border">
    <p><span class="highlight4">Email Delivery:</span> Many mail servers reject email from IPs without valid PTR records. If your mail server's IP doesn't reverse-resolve to your domain, your emails may be flagged as spam.</p>
    <p><span class="highlight4">Security Logging:</span> PTR lookups identify which hosts were involved in network events — logs showing <code>webserver.company.com</code> are more useful than raw IPs.</p>
    <p><span class="highlight4">SSH & Services:</span> Some services perform reverse DNS lookups for access control and auditing purposes.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 10: MX -->
<section id="mx">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(56,189,248,0.12);color:var(--accent);">📧</span> MX Record (Mail Exchange)</h2>

  <p>The <span class="highlight">MX record</span> tells the internet where to deliver email for a domain. Each MX record has a <span class="highlight5">priority value</span> (lower = preferred). When sending email to <code>user@example.com</code>, the sending server queries MX records for <code>example.com</code> to find the mail server.</p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">mx-records.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="comment">; MX records with priority (lower = higher priority)</span>
<span class="key">example.com.</span>  <span class="val">3600</span>  IN  MX  <span class="val">10</span>  <span class="cmd">mail1.example.com.</span>   <span class="comment">; primary</span>
<span class="key">example.com.</span>  <span class="val">3600</span>  IN  MX  <span class="val">20</span>  <span class="cmd">mail2.example.com.</span>   <span class="comment">; secondary</span>
<span class="key">example.com.</span>  <span class="val">3600</span>  IN  MX  <span class="val">30</span>  <span class="cmd">backup.example.com.</span>  <span class="comment">; tertiary</span>

<span class="comment">; Google Workspace MX records</span>
<span class="key">company.com.</span>  <span class="val">3600</span>  IN  MX  <span class="val">1</span>   <span class="cmd">aspmx.l.google.com.</span>
<span class="key">company.com.</span>  <span class="val">3600</span>  IN  MX  <span class="val">5</span>   <span class="cmd">alt1.aspmx.l.google.com.</span>
<span class="key">company.com.</span>  <span class="val">3600</span>  IN  MX  <span class="val">5</span>   <span class="cmd">alt2.aspmx.l.google.com.</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 11: CNAME, TXT, SRV -->
<section id="cname">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(129,140,248,0.12);color:var(--accent2);">📎</span> CNAME, TXT, SRV & More</h2>

  <h3>CNAME (Canonical Name)</h3>
  <p>An alias that points one domain name to another. When a resolver hits a CNAME, it follows the chain to the canonical name and looks up its A/AAAA record. <span class="highlight3">Important:</span> You cannot have a CNAME at the zone apex (e.g., <code>example.com</code>) alongside other records — it's only for subdomains.</p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">other-records.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="comment">; CNAME — aliases</span>
<span class="key">www</span>       IN  CNAME  <span class="cmd">example.com.</span>
<span class="key">blog</span>      IN  CNAME  <span class="cmd">mysite.wordpress.com.</span>
<span class="key">shop</span>      IN  CNAME  <span class="cmd">shops.shopify.com.</span>

<span class="comment">; TXT — verification, email auth</span>
<span class="key">@</span>         IN  TXT    <span class="cmd">"v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com ~all"</span>
<span class="key">@</span>         IN  TXT    <span class="cmd">"google-site-verification=abc123..."</span>
<span class="key">_dmarc</span>    IN  TXT    <span class="cmd">"v=DMARC1; p=reject; rua=mailto:dmarc@example.com"</span>

<span class="comment">; SRV — service locator (format: priority weight port target)</span>
<span class="key">_sip._tcp.example.com.</span>  IN  SRV  <span class="val">10 60 5060</span>  <span class="cmd">sipserver.example.com.</span>
<span class="key">_ldap._tcp.example.com.</span> IN  SRV  <span class="val">0 100 389</span>  <span class="cmd">ldap.example.com.</span>

<span class="comment">; CAA — Certificate Authority Authorization</span>
<span class="key">@</span>         IN  CAA    <span class="val">0 issue</span> <span class="cmd">"letsencrypt.org"</span>
<span class="key">@</span>         IN  CAA    <span class="val">0 issuewild</span> <span class="cmd">";"</span>  <span class="comment">; block wildcard certs</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="separator">

<!-- SECTION 12: DIG -->
<section id="dig">
  <h2><span class="icon" style="background:rgba(52,211,153,0.12);color:var(--accent4);">🔧</span> Using <code>dig</code> — The DNS Swiss Army Knife</h2>

  <p><span class="highlight4"><code>dig</code></span> (Domain Information Groper) is the go-to command-line tool for querying DNS. It comes with the <code>bind-utils</code> / <code>dnsutils</code> package on Linux.</p>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">dig-commands.sh</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="comment"># ─── Basic Queries ─────────────────────────────────────</span>

<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig google.com</span>              <span class="comment"># Default: query A record</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig google.com A</span>            <span class="comment"># Explicit A record query</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig google.com AAAA</span>         <span class="comment"># IPv6 address</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig google.com MX</span>           <span class="comment"># Mail servers</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig google.com NS</span>           <span class="comment"># Name servers</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig google.com SOA</span>          <span class="comment"># Start of Authority</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig google.com TXT</span>          <span class="comment"># TXT records (SPF, etc.)</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig google.com ANY</span>          <span class="comment"># All records (often blocked)</span>

<span class="comment"># ─── Reverse DNS Lookup ────────────────────────────────</span>

<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig -x 8.8.8.8</span>              <span class="comment"># PTR lookup for IP</span>
<span class="comment"># Equivalent to: dig 8.8.8.8.in-addr.arpa. PTR</span>

<span class="comment"># ─── Using a Specific DNS Server ───────────────────────</span>

<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig @8.8.8.8 google.com</span>     <span class="comment"># Query Google's DNS</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig @1.1.1.1 google.com</span>     <span class="comment"># Query Cloudflare's DNS</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig @ns1.google.com google.com</span>  <span class="comment"># Direct authoritative query</span>

<span class="comment"># ─── Short Output ──────────────────────────────────────</span>

<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig +short google.com</span>       <span class="comment"># Just the IP, nothing else</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig +short google.com MX</span>    <span class="comment"># Just MX data</span>

<span class="comment"># ─── Trace the Full Resolution Path ────────────────────</span>

<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig +trace google.com</span>       <span class="comment"># Show root → TLD → auth chain</span>
<span class="comment"># This is AMAZING for debugging — shows every step of resolution</span>

<span class="comment"># ─── Check TTL & Caching ───────────────────────────────</span>

<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig google.com | grep -A1 "ANSWER SECTION"</span>
<span class="comment"># The number after the domain is the TTL in seconds</span>
<span class="comment"># Run it twice — watch the TTL count down (cached!)</span>

<span class="comment"># ─── Zone Transfer Attempt ─────────────────────────────</span>

<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig @ns1.example.com example.com AXFR</span>
<span class="comment"># Full zone transfer — usually blocked for security</span>

<span class="comment"># ─── Useful Flags ──────────────────────────────────────</span>

<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig +noall +answer google.com</span>       <span class="comment"># Only answer section</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig +noall +authority google.com</span>    <span class="comment"># Only authority section</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig +norecurse @8.8.8.8 google.com</span> <span class="comment"># Non-recursive query</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig +stats google.com</span>               <span class="comment"># Show query time stats</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig +multiline google.com SOA</span>       <span class="comment"># Pretty-print SOA fields</span></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Reading dig Output</h3>

  <div class="terminal">
    <div class="terminal-header">
      <div class="terminal-dot r"></div><div class="terminal-dot y"></div><div class="terminal-dot g"></div>
      <span class="terminal-title">dig-output-explained.txt</span>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-body"><span class="prompt">$</span> <span class="cmd">dig example.com</span>

<span class="comment">; &lt;&lt;&gt;&gt; DiG 9.18.18 &lt;&lt;&gt;&gt; example.com</span>
<span class="comment">;; global options: +cmd</span>

<span class="section-head">;; HEADER</span>
<span class="comment">;; ->>HEADER<<- opcode: QUERY, status: NOERROR, id: 12345</span>
<span class="comment">;;                                          ↑ NOERROR = success</span>
<span class="comment">;;                                            NXDOMAIN = domain doesn't exist</span>
<span class="comment">;;                                            SERVFAIL = server error</span>
<span class="comment">;; flags: qr rd ra; QUERY: 1, ANSWER: 1, AUTHORITY: 0</span>
<span class="comment">;;         ↑  ↑  ↑</span>
<span class="comment">;;         │  │  └── ra = Recursion Available</span>
<span class="comment">;;         │  └───── rd = Recursion Desired</span>
<span class="comment">;;         └──────── qr = Query Response</span>

<span class="section-head">;; QUESTION SECTION:</span>
<span class="key">;example.com.</span>                  IN      A
<span class="comment">;; ^ What we asked for</span>

